# Run artefacts. Every suite creates its own data dir, log, screenshots and
# most of its fixtures beside itself; none of that belongs in the repo.
#
# THE EXCEPTIONS ARE THE THREE CI FIXTURES, which ARE committed (see below)
# because CI installs no browser and cannot print them.
*-data/
*-data-*/
*-shots/
*shots/
gib/
*.log
*.png
*.xlsx
*.pdf
oc-pdfs/

# …except these two. perf-e2e.js and perf-ocr-e2e.js read them and neither
# generates one, so a fresh checkout without them is a red CI run. They are
# printed by mk-multipage.js / mk-scanned.js, which need Chromium — and
# perf-e2e is an API suite whose whole point is measuring the PDF worker, so
# making it launch a browser just to build its own input would be backwards.
# Deterministic, 128 KB the pair, regenerate with the two mk-*.js scripts.
!perf-12.pdf
!perf-scan3.pdf

# …and the TracXLogis label fixture, for the same reason: tracx-automatch-e2e
# is an API suite in CI, and printing its own input would drag a Chromium
# install into a job that needs none. It carries INVENTED consignees — a real
# carrier label carries a customer's name and address and is never committed.
# Regenerate with `node tracx-fixture.js tracx-fixture.pdf`.
!tracx-fixture.pdf

# …and the pair tracx-consignee-e2e.js reads (a Keyfields-shaped picking list
# with the waybill in the Consignee box, and a 3-page TracX label), same
# reasoning, same invented names. Regenerate with
# `node tracx-consignee-fixture.js`.
!tracx-picklist-fixture.pdf
!tracx-label2-fixture.pdf
